1
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:58,950
Dismount!

2
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:07,450
Herman's horse-sick!

3
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,450
Chaps, no one wander off!

4
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:21,850
Some of the passageways in here can run for miles.

5
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:34,750
I don't think this is such a good idea.

6
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:40,150
What is it?

7
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:06,550
Alfred, did you get anything yet?

8
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:07,650
Nothing.

9
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:10,150
The kid's got something.

10
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:15,650
I got something, Fedora!  I got something right here.

11
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:24,450
Oh, look at that!

12
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:27,850
- We're rich!  We're rich!   - Shut up!

13
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:32,650
Indy?

14
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:36,550
Indy?  What are they doing?

15
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:39,250
Indiana?  Indiana...?

16
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:46,350
We gotta find more stuff.

17
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:48,750
It's the Cross of Coronado.

18
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:52,750
Cort gave it to him in 1520.

19
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:57,750
I'll give that ring to my mama and put it on her bony finger!

20
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:02,950
That cross is an important artefact, it belongs in a museum.

21
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:06,950
Run back, tell Mr Havelock there are men looting in the caves.

22
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:10,750
Have him bring the sheriff. It's only a snake!

23
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:11,750
Did you hear me?

24
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:15,650
Right. Run back. Mr Havelock. The sheriff...

25
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:17,950
What are you gonna do?

26
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,950
I don't know. I'll think of something.

27
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:28,650
Dig with your hands, not your mouth!

28
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:47,150
- He's got our thing.  - Get him!

29
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:55,050
Mr Havelock!  Anybody!

30
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:57,150
Everybody's lost but me.

31
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,850
There he is!  Let's go!

32
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:18,250
Hey, you!  We're gonna get you!

33
00:05:23,550 --> 00:05:25,250
Hey!  Come back here!

34
00:05:39,150 --> 00:05:40,260
Come on!  Get him!

35
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:49,550
Come back here!

36
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:58,350
Here, let me!

37
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:38,450
Holy smoke!

38
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:04,150
Come on, kid.

39
00:08:05,850 --> 00:08:07,250
There's no way out of this.

40
00:08:40,450 --> 00:08:42,850
Toss up the whip!

41
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:54,450
You got heart, kid, but that belongs to me.

42
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:56,450
It belongs to Coronado.

43
00:08:56,450 --> 00:08:58,450
Coronado is dead, and so are all of his grandchildren.

44
00:08:58,850 --> 00:09:00,950
This should be in a museum.

45
00:09:01,150 --> 00:09:03,050
Give me that!

46
00:09:05,950 --> 00:09:08,050
A snake!

47
00:09:08,250 --> 00:09:10,750
Don't let him get away!

48
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:12,850
Magic?

49
00:09:14,650 --> 00:09:17,350
Make sure he doesn't double back.

50
00:09:33,850 --> 00:09:37,750
OK, kid, out of the box, now!

51
00:09:38,150 --> 00:09:39,950
Son of a bitch!

52
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:52,450
Damn!

53
00:10:03,550 --> 00:10:04,850
Dad!

54
00:10:09,750 --> 00:10:11,050
Dad!

55
00:10:12,550 --> 00:10:15,850
-Dad! It's important.  -Wait. Count to twenty.

56
00:10:16,050 --> 00:10:18,150
- No, listen to me...  - Junior!

57
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:20,350
One, two, three, four...

58
00:10:20,650 --> 00:10:23,350
In Greek.

59
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:40,550
May he who illuminated this illuminate me.

60
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:46,250
I brought the sheriff.

61
00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:50,150
Just the man I want to see.

62
00:10:50,350 --> 00:10:54,050
- Five or six of them...  - All right. You still got it?

63
00:10:54,250 --> 00:10:56,650
Well, yes...  it's right here.

64
00:10:57,450 --> 00:11:00,050
I'm glad to see that.

65
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:02,250
because the rightful owner of this cross

66
00:11:02,350 --> 00:11:06,050
won't press charges if you give it back.

67
00:11:06,250 --> 00:11:09,250
He's got witnesses, five or six of them.

68
00:11:32,450 --> 00:11:34,550
Good day.

69
00:11:38,450 --> 00:11:41,250
You lost today, kid.

70
00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:44,050
But it doesn't mean you have to like it.

71
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:16,150
- Small world, Dr Jones.  - Too small for two of us.

72
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:26,950
I've had to reclaim my property from you before.

73
00:12:27,250 --> 00:12:29,950
That belongs in a museum!

74
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:31,950
So do you!

75
00:12:32,250 --> 00:12:34,850
Throw him over the side!

76
00:13:22,450 --> 00:13:24,750
Grab him, he's getting away!

77
00:14:18,750 --> 00:14:22,950
Archaeology is the search for fact...

78
00:14:25,050 --> 00:14:26,650
...not truth.

79
00:14:26,850 --> 00:14:31,950
If it's truth you're interested in, the philosophy class is down the hall.

80
00:14:32,150 --> 00:14:36,950
Forget lost cities, exotic travel and digging up the world.

81
00:14:37,150 --> 00:14:42,350
We don't follow maps to treasure, and "X" never ever marks the spot.

82
00:14:45,350 --> 00:14:49,250
Seventy per cent of all archaeology is done in the library.

83
00:14:49,450 --> 00:14:51,550
Research, reading...

84
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:57,350
We cannot afford to take mythology at face value.

85
00:14:58,750 --> 00:15:04,150
Next week: Egyptology and the excavation of Naukratis of 1885.

86
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:08,050
I'll be in my office if anybody's got problems.

87
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:16,950
Marcus... I did it.

88
00:15:17,150 --> 00:15:19,150
You've got it?

89
00:15:28,850 --> 00:15:33,450
- Know how long I've looked for that?  - All your life.

90
00:15:33,650 --> 00:15:37,650
- All my life.  - Well done, very well done indeed.

91
00:15:37,850 --> 00:15:41,250
This will find a place of honour in our Spanish collection.

92
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:45,350
We can discuss my honorarium over dinner and champagne.

93
00:15:45,650 --> 00:15:50,150
-Your treat. -Yes... my treat.

94
00:15:53,150 --> 00:15:54,950
Dr Jones!

95
00:16:02,250 --> 00:16:06,650
Your mail is on your desk. Here are your phone messages.

96
00:16:06,850 --> 00:16:10,750
This is your schedule. These papers still need grading.

97
00:16:10,950 --> 00:16:14,550
Irene, put everybody's name on a list...

98
00:16:15,950 --> 00:16:20,350
...and I'll see each and every one of them in turn!

99
00:16:32,250 --> 00:16:34,350
"Venice, Italy."

100
00:16:34,550 --> 00:16:37,350
Dr Jones!  Dr Jones!

101
00:17:03,350 --> 00:17:04,750
Dr Jones!

102
00:17:10,650 --> 00:17:12,150
Dr Jones.

103
00:17:28,150 --> 00:17:31,850
I trust your trip down was comfortable, Dr Jones.

104
00:17:32,250 --> 00:17:35,150
My men didn't alarm you, I hope.

105
00:17:35,450 --> 00:17:37,550
My name is Walter Donovan.

106
00:17:37,750 --> 00:17:42,550
I know you. Your contributions to the museum have been generous.

107
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:45,050
Your collection is very impressive.

108
00:17:45,250 --> 00:17:49,150
Like yourself, I have a passion for antiquities.

109
00:17:49,350 --> 00:17:51,250
Have a look over here.

110
00:17:52,750 --> 00:17:55,650
This might interest you.

111
00:17:57,750 --> 00:18:00,850
Well, it's sandstone. Christian symbol.

112
00:18:01,850 --> 00:18:05,550
Early Latin text. Mid-12th century.

113
00:18:05,850 --> 00:18:08,750
- That was our assessment, too.  - Where's it from?

114
00:18:08,950 --> 00:18:14,050
My engineers unearthed it north of Ankara, while excavating for copper.

115
00:18:14,250 --> 00:18:17,050
Can you translate the inscription?

116
00:18:19,850 --> 00:18:23,650
"'... who drinks the water I shall give him,' says the Lord,

117
00:18:23,850 --> 00:18:31,750
"'will have a spring inside him welling up for eternal life.'

118
00:18:31,950 --> 00:18:35,150
"Let them bring me to your Holy Mountain where you dwell,

119
00:18:35,350 --> 00:18:39,350
"across the desert, through the mountain,

120
00:18:39,550 --> 00:18:42,550
"to the Canyon of the Crescent Moon,

121
00:18:42,950 --> 00:18:45,750
"to the temple where the cup that...

122
00:18:48,850 --> 00:18:54,150
"...where the cup that holds the blood of Jesus Christ resides for ever."

123
00:18:54,850 --> 00:18:58,250
The Holy Grail, Dr Jones.

124
00:18:58,450 --> 00:19:02,450
The chalice used by Christ during the Last Supper,

125
00:19:02,650 --> 00:19:05,550
that caught his blood at the Crucifixion

126
00:19:05,750 --> 00:19:08,950
and was entrusted to Joseph of Arimathea.

127
00:19:09,250 --> 00:19:13,850
The Arthur Legend. I've heard this bedtime story before.

128
00:19:14,050 --> 00:19:18,650
Eternal life!  The gift of youth to whoever drinks from the Grail.

129
00:19:18,850 --> 00:19:22,450
- That's a bedtime story I like!  - An old man's dream.

130
00:19:22,650 --> 00:19:27,250
Every man's dream, including your father's, I believe.

131
00:19:30,050 --> 00:19:32,150
Grail lore is his hobby.

132
00:19:32,350 --> 00:19:36,650
He's the teacher of Medieval Literature the students hope they don't get.

133
00:19:36,950 --> 00:19:39,150
You're neglecting your guests.

134
00:19:39,350 --> 00:19:41,350
Along in a moment, dear.

135
00:19:46,950 --> 00:19:52,650
Hard to resist, isn't it?  The Grail's final resting place described in detail.

136
00:19:52,850 --> 00:19:58,450
What good is it?  This tablet speaks of deserts, mountains, canyons...

137
00:19:58,750 --> 00:20:01,350
Pretty vague. Where do you start?

138
00:20:01,550 --> 00:20:05,550
If the tablet were intact, but the top portion is missing.

139
00:20:05,750 --> 00:20:11,450
Just the same, an attempt to recover the Grail is currently underway.

140
00:20:11,750 --> 00:20:14,950
Let me tell you another bedtime story.

141
00:20:15,250 --> 00:20:20,950
After the Grail was entrusted to Joseph, it was lost for a thousand years,

142
00:20:21,150 --> 00:20:26,250
before it was found again by three brothers in the First Crusade.

143
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:28,350
I've heard this one, too.

144
00:20:28,650 --> 00:20:33,750
Two of the brothers walked out of the desert 150 years later

145
00:20:33,950 --> 00:20:37,950
and journeyed back to France, but only one made it.

146
00:20:38,150 --> 00:20:41,250
Before dying of... extreme old age,

147
00:20:41,450 --> 00:20:46,450
he supposedly imparted his tale to a Franciscan friar.

148
00:20:46,650 --> 00:20:49,350
Not "supposedly", Dr Jones.

149
00:20:49,550 --> 00:20:54,550
This is the manuscript in which the friar chronicled the knight's story.

150
00:20:54,850 --> 00:20:57,050
It doesn't reveal the Grail's location,

151
00:20:57,350 --> 00:21:00,650
but the knight promised that two markers would.

152
00:21:01,250 --> 00:21:04,250
This tablet is one of those markers.

153
00:21:04,650 --> 00:21:09,150
It proves the knight's story is true, but it's incomplete.

154
00:21:09,350 --> 00:21:12,350
The second marker is in his dead brother's tomb.

155
00:21:12,550 --> 00:21:18,150
Our project leader believes that tomb is located in Venice, Italy.

156
00:21:20,550 --> 00:21:25,550
As you can now see, we're about to complete a great quest

157
00:21:25,850 --> 00:21:28,850
that began almost two thousand years ago.

158
00:21:29,150 --> 00:21:31,250
We're only one step away.

159
00:21:31,550 --> 00:21:35,450
That's usually when the ground falls out from under you.

160
00:21:35,750 --> 00:21:38,650
You could be more right than you know.

161
00:21:38,950 --> 00:21:41,450
We've hit a snag.

162
00:21:41,750 --> 00:21:46,450
Our project leader has vanished, along with all his research.

163
00:21:46,650 --> 00:21:52,350
His colleague, Dr Schneider, has no idea of his whereabouts.

164
00:21:53,350 --> 00:21:57,050
I want you to pick up the trail where he left off.

165
00:21:57,250 --> 00:22:00,550
Find the man, and you will find the Grail.

166
00:22:01,750 --> 00:22:05,950
You've got the wrong Jones, Mr Donovan.

167
00:22:06,250 --> 00:22:09,950
- Why don't you try my father?  - We already have.

168
00:22:10,150 --> 00:22:13,250
Your father is the man who's disappeared.

169
00:22:24,950 --> 00:22:29,550
Your father and I have been friends for years,

170
00:22:29,750 --> 00:22:32,250
and I've never seen you this concerned about him before.

171
00:22:32,250 --> 00:22:33,000
Dad?

172
00:22:33,550 --> 00:22:37,150
He's an academic, not a field man. Dad?

173
00:22:37,350 --> 00:22:38,650
Dad?

174
00:22:38,950 --> 00:22:40,650
Dear God!

175
00:22:43,050 --> 00:22:47,050
What's the old fool got himself into now?

176
00:22:47,450 --> 00:22:51,850
I don't know, but whatever it is, he's in over his head.

177
00:22:52,650 --> 00:22:56,150
It's today's mail, and it's been opened.

178
00:22:58,050 --> 00:22:59,550
Mail...

179
00:22:59,850 --> 00:23:02,850
That's it, Marcus.

180
00:23:03,750 --> 00:23:05,450
"Venice, Italy."

181
00:23:08,750 --> 00:23:10,750
What is it?

182
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:15,750
It's Dad's Grail diary.

183
00:23:17,250 --> 00:23:20,150
Every clue he followed, every discovery he made.

184
00:23:20,350 --> 00:23:24,450
A complete record of his search for the Holy Grail.

185
00:23:24,750 --> 00:23:29,550
This is his whole life. Why would he have sent this to me?

186
00:23:29,750 --> 00:23:34,650
I don't know... but someone must want it pretty badly.

187
00:23:43,950 --> 00:23:46,450
Do you believe, Marcus?

188
00:23:57,850 --> 00:24:01,150
Do you believe the Grail actually exists?

189
00:24:02,150 --> 00:24:06,550
The search for it is the search for the divine in all of us.

190
00:24:06,850 --> 00:24:10,750
But if you want facts, I've none to give you.

191
00:24:11,050 --> 00:24:15,250
At my age, I'm prepared to take a few things on faith.

192
00:24:20,450 --> 00:24:25,950
Call Donovan, tell him I'll take that ticket to Venice now.

193
00:24:26,150 --> 00:24:28,150
I'll tell him we'll take two.

194
00:24:33,350 --> 00:24:37,750
- What'll happen in Ven...?  - Dr Schneider will meet you.

195
00:24:37,950 --> 00:24:41,350
My apartment in Venice is at your disposal.

196
00:24:41,550 --> 00:24:42,950
Thank you.

197
00:24:44,850 --> 00:24:47,750
Dr Jones... good luck.

198
00:24:47,950 --> 00:24:51,050
Be very careful. Don't trust anybody.

199
00:25:34,650 --> 00:25:36,750
Ah, Venice.

200
00:25:38,050 --> 00:25:41,950
How will we recognise Dr Schneider?

201
00:25:42,150 --> 00:25:44,650
I don't know. Maybe he'll know us.

202
00:25:44,850 --> 00:25:46,550
Dr Jones?

203
00:25:47,850 --> 00:25:49,450
Yes.

204
00:25:49,650 --> 00:25:54,050
I knew it was you. You have your father's eyes.

205
00:25:56,150 --> 00:25:59,350
And my mother's ears, but the rest is yours.

206
00:25:59,650 --> 00:26:03,350
It looks like the best parts are spoken for.

207
00:26:03,650 --> 00:26:05,950
- Marcus Brody?  - That's right.

208
00:26:06,150 --> 00:26:08,650
- Dr Elsa Schneider.  - How do you do?

209
00:26:09,650 --> 00:26:12,350
I last saw your father in the library.

210
00:26:12,650 --> 00:26:17,050
He was very close to tracking down the tomb. He was so excited.

211
00:26:17,450 --> 00:26:21,550
- He was as giddy as a schoolboy.  - Attila the Professor?

212
00:26:21,750 --> 00:26:25,550
He was never giddy, even as a schoolboy.

213
00:26:25,750 --> 00:26:28,450
- Frlein, will you permit me?  - I usually don't.

214
00:26:28,750 --> 00:26:32,250
- Me neither. - Then I permit you.

215
00:26:32,450 --> 00:26:35,450
- But by tomorrow it will have faded.  - Tomorrow I'll...

216
00:26:35,750 --> 00:26:40,150
- I hate to interrupt, but the reason...  - I have something to show you.

217
00:26:40,350 --> 00:26:45,150
Your father sent me to get an ancient plan of the city.

218
00:26:45,350 --> 00:26:51,050
When I got back, he'd gone, with all his papers except that scrap.

219
00:26:52,450 --> 00:26:54,550
- Roman numerals.  - Here is the library.

220
00:26:54,750 --> 00:26:59,350
- That doesn't look like a library.  - Looks like a converted church.

221
00:26:59,650 --> 00:27:03,250
It's the literal truth. You're on holy ground.

222
00:27:03,550 --> 00:27:07,050
These columns were brought back as spoils of war,

223
00:27:07,250 --> 00:27:10,350
after the sacking of Byzantium in the Crusades.

224
00:27:12,250 --> 00:27:18,250
The library's closing soon. I'll arrange for us to stay a little longer.

225
00:27:20,950 --> 00:27:24,650
Marcus, I've seen this window before.

226
00:27:24,850 --> 00:27:28,950
- Where?  - Right here. In Dad's diary.

227
00:27:36,550 --> 00:27:41,050
- You see?  - Look, Indy. The Roman numerals.

228
00:27:41,250 --> 00:27:47,050
- Dad was onto something here.  - But we don't know what they mean.

229
00:27:47,250 --> 00:27:51,450
He sent me the diary for a reason. Let's keep it to ourselves.

230
00:27:51,650 --> 00:27:54,650
- Find something?  - Three, seven and ten.

231
00:27:54,950 --> 00:27:59,050
- The numerals are on the window.  - I must be blind.

232
00:27:59,350 --> 00:28:05,850
He wasn't looking for a book about the tomb, but for the tomb itself.

233
00:28:06,050 --> 00:28:10,750
The tomb is in the library. You said it used to be a church. Look!

234
00:28:11,450 --> 00:28:12,750
Three.

235
00:28:15,050 --> 00:28:16,250
Three.

236
00:28:16,550 --> 00:28:18,650
Seven. Seven.

237
00:28:18,850 --> 00:28:22,250
Ten... and ten.

238
00:28:22,450 --> 00:28:24,850
Where's the ten?

239
00:28:25,850 --> 00:28:28,350
Look around for the ten.

240
00:28:36,950 --> 00:28:38,650
Three and seven...

241
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:42,650
Three and seven and ten...

242
00:29:04,050 --> 00:29:07,950
Ten!  "X" marks the spot.

243
00:30:06,050 --> 00:30:08,550
- Bingo!  - You don't disappoint, Dr Jones.

244
00:30:08,850 --> 00:30:11,750
- You're just like your father.  - Except he's lost and I'm not.

245
00:30:12,150 --> 00:30:14,050
Lower me down.

246
00:30:23,350 --> 00:30:26,350
Look after this for me, will you?

247
00:30:39,850 --> 00:30:41,450
Come on!

248
00:30:48,750 --> 00:30:52,650
- Pagan symbols, 4th or 5th century.  - Right.

249
00:30:52,950 --> 00:30:55,750
Six hundred years before the Crusades.

250
00:30:56,150 --> 00:30:59,950
The Christians dug their own passages centuries later.

251
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:05,350
If there's a knight of the First Crusade here, that's where we'll find him.

252
00:31:37,750 --> 00:31:40,850
- What's this one?  - The Ark of the Covenant.

253
00:31:41,050 --> 00:31:44,050
- Are you sure?  - Pretty sure.

254
00:32:00,850 --> 00:32:02,450
Watch out.

255
00:32:17,950 --> 00:32:23,050
It's petroleum. I should sink a well down here and retire!

256
00:32:38,650 --> 00:32:39,850
The lighter.

257
00:32:50,450 --> 00:32:52,550
Oh, rats...!

258
00:33:08,350 --> 00:33:09,950
Come on.

259
00:33:58,450 --> 00:34:00,050
Come here.

260
00:34:07,950 --> 00:34:11,050
Look, it must be one of these.

261
00:34:11,350 --> 00:34:15,450
Look at the artistry of these carvings and the scrollwork.

262
00:34:31,450 --> 00:34:33,950
It's this one.

263
00:34:48,450 --> 00:34:52,250
This is it. We've found it!

264
00:34:54,850 --> 00:34:56,650
Look!

265
00:34:56,850 --> 00:35:02,850
The engraving on the shield is the same as on the Grail tablet.

266
00:35:03,050 --> 00:35:05,450
The shield is the second marker!

267
00:35:05,650 --> 00:35:07,150
What's that?

268
00:35:07,450 --> 00:35:11,450
It's a rubbing Dad made of the Grail tablet.

269
00:35:12,150 --> 00:35:16,450
Just like your father, giddy as a schoolboy!

270
00:35:16,650 --> 00:35:20,750
Wouldn't it be wonderful if he were here to see this?

271
00:35:20,950 --> 00:35:26,150
He'd never have made it past the rats. He hates rats.

272
00:35:48,050 --> 00:35:50,250
Get back against the wall!

273
00:35:55,150 --> 00:35:58,150
Quick, under it!  Air pocket!

274
00:36:03,350 --> 00:36:06,850
- Don't wander off.  - What? What?!

275
00:36:25,950 --> 00:36:29,950
I think I've found a way out. Deep breath!

276
00:36:46,250 --> 00:36:48,450
Ah... Venice!

277
00:37:54,650 --> 00:37:59,550
- Are you crazy?  Not between them!  - Between them?  Are you crazy?

278
00:38:10,450 --> 00:38:14,150
- I said go around!  - You said between them!

279
00:38:14,450 --> 00:38:17,950
I said don't go between them!

280
00:39:19,050 --> 00:39:21,250
No!

281
00:39:26,050 --> 00:39:29,250
- Why kill us?   - Because you're looking for the Grail.

282
00:39:29,950 --> 00:39:34,050
- Did you kill my father, too?  - No!

283
00:39:34,350 --> 00:39:36,350
Where is he?

284
00:39:36,650 --> 00:39:41,750
Talk or you're dead!  Dammit, tell me!  Tell me!

285
00:39:41,950 --> 00:39:45,850
- If you don't let go, we'll both die!  - Then we'll die!

286
00:39:46,050 --> 00:39:48,950
My soul is prepared. How's yours?

287
00:39:50,850 --> 00:39:55,150
- This is your last chance!  - No, Dr Jones, it's yours.

288
00:40:11,250 --> 00:40:13,550
All right, where's my father?

289
00:40:13,750 --> 00:40:16,450
If you let me go, I will tell you.

290
00:40:17,350 --> 00:40:20,250
- Who are you?  - My name is Kazim.

291
00:40:20,650 --> 00:40:22,550
Why did you try to kill me?

292
00:40:22,750 --> 00:40:26,850
The secret of the Grail has been safe for a thousand years.

293
00:40:27,150 --> 00:40:30,850
All that time, the Brotherhood of the Cruciform Sword

294
00:40:31,050 --> 00:40:35,550
have been prepared to do anything to keep it safe.

295
00:40:36,750 --> 00:40:38,750
Let me off at this jetty.

296
00:40:41,950 --> 00:40:46,950
Ask yourself, why do you seek the Cup of Christ?

297
00:40:47,150 --> 00:40:50,850
Is it for His glory... or for yours?

298
00:40:51,050 --> 00:40:55,750
I didn't come for the Cup of Christ, I came to find my father.

299
00:40:55,950 --> 00:40:58,650
In that case, God be with you.

300
00:40:58,850 --> 00:41:03,750
He's held in the Castle of Brunwald on the Austrian-German border.

301
00:41:12,150 --> 00:41:16,550
- How's the head?  - Better, now I've seen this.

302
00:41:16,850 --> 00:41:21,150
It's the name of a city: Alexandretta.

303
00:41:23,650 --> 00:41:28,350
The knights of the First Crusade laid siege to Alexandretta.

304
00:41:28,650 --> 00:41:31,650
The entire city was destroyed.

305
00:41:33,550 --> 00:41:38,150
The present city of Iskenderun is built on its ruins.

306
00:41:38,350 --> 00:41:42,250
Remember what the Grail tablet said?  "Across the desert,

307
00:41:42,450 --> 00:41:46,850
"through the mountain, to the Canyon of the Crescent Moon."

308
00:41:48,950 --> 00:41:53,050
- Where, exactly?  - Your father would know.

309
00:41:54,450 --> 00:41:57,950
Your father did know!  Look, he made a map!

310
00:41:58,350 --> 00:42:02,250
He pieced it together from clues in the Grail quest.

311
00:42:02,450 --> 00:42:07,250
A map with no names. A city with an oasis due east... here.

312
00:42:07,450 --> 00:42:13,550
He knew the course turns south to the mountains, straight to the canyon!

313
00:42:13,750 --> 00:42:18,850
He knew everything except where to begin: the name of the city.

314
00:42:19,850 --> 00:42:23,750
- Alexandretta. Now we know. - Yes, now we know.

315
00:42:24,050 --> 00:42:26,550
Tell Sallah to meet you in Iskenderun.

316
00:42:26,750 --> 00:42:28,350
What about you?

317
00:42:28,550 --> 00:42:30,750
I'm going after Dad.

318
00:42:51,450 --> 00:42:52,750
Elsa?

319
00:42:58,550 --> 00:43:00,650
Elsa?

320
00:43:11,950 --> 00:43:14,650
- My room! - Mine, too.

321
00:43:14,850 --> 00:43:17,750
- What were they looking for?  - This.

322
00:43:18,050 --> 00:43:20,850
The Grail diary.

323
00:43:21,050 --> 00:43:23,150
You had it?

324
00:43:23,450 --> 00:43:27,050
- You didn't trust me.  - I didn't know you.

325
00:43:27,350 --> 00:43:29,650
At least I let you tag along.

326
00:43:29,850 --> 00:43:35,050
Yes, give them a flower and they'll follow you anywhere.

327
00:43:35,250 --> 00:43:39,050
- Knock it off. You're not mad.  - No?

328
00:43:39,250 --> 00:43:41,950
No. You like the way I do things.

329
00:43:42,150 --> 00:43:47,850
Lucky I don't do things the same way or you'd still be standing at the pier!

330
00:43:48,250 --> 00:43:49,950
What's going on there?

331
00:43:50,150 --> 00:43:55,050
I've nearly been incinerated, drowned and chopped into fish bait.

332
00:43:55,250 --> 00:43:59,650
We're caught in something sinister here, and my Dad found out more than is looking for.

333
00:43:59,850 --> 00:44:05,850
Until I'm sure, I'll do things the way I think they should be done.

334
00:44:08,850 --> 00:44:11,650
How dare you kiss me!

335
00:44:17,050 --> 00:44:20,650
Leave me alone. I don't like fast women.

336
00:44:21,550 --> 00:44:25,350
And I hate arrogant men.

337
00:44:35,150 --> 00:44:37,250
I love Venice...

338
00:45:08,950 --> 00:45:10,250
What do you know about this place?

339
00:45:10,750 --> 00:45:14,250
I know the Brunwalds are art collectors.

340
00:45:16,250 --> 00:45:17,350
What are you going to do?

341
00:45:17,370 --> 00:45:20,250
I don't know. I'll think of something.

342
00:45:28,550 --> 00:45:30,650
- Yes?  - Not before time.

343
00:45:31,050 --> 00:45:33,250
Did you intend to leave us standing on the doorstep?

344
00:45:33,350 --> 00:45:35,250
We're drenched.

345
00:45:36,450 --> 00:45:38,750
I've gone and caught a sniffle.

346
00:45:39,050 --> 00:45:40,850
Are you expected?

347
00:45:40,850 --> 00:45:42,850
Do not take that tone with me.

348
00:45:43,250 --> 00:45:48,450
Tell the Baron that Lord Clarence MacDonald and his assistant

349
00:45:48,750 --> 00:45:51,050
are here to view the tapestries.

350
00:45:51,250 --> 00:45:53,750
- Tapestries?  - The man is dense...!

351
00:45:53,950 --> 00:45:57,750
This is a castle, isn't it?  There are tapestries?

352
00:45:57,950 --> 00:46:02,050
This is a castle. And we have many tapestries.

353
00:46:02,350 --> 00:46:07,750
And if you are a Scottish lord, then I am Mickey Mouse!

354
00:46:07,950 --> 00:46:09,750
How dare he?

355
00:46:36,450 --> 00:46:39,350
Nazis. I hate these guys.

356
00:46:52,650 --> 00:46:55,550
- I think he's in here.  - How do you know?

357
00:46:55,750 --> 00:46:57,950
Because it's wired.

358
00:47:25,950 --> 00:47:28,450
Indy?  Indy!

359
00:47:28,650 --> 00:47:32,550
Don't worry, this is kid's play. I'll be right back.

360
00:47:57,050 --> 00:47:59,350
Junior?

361
00:47:59,650 --> 00:48:03,050
- Yes, sir.  - It is you, Junior.

362
00:48:03,250 --> 00:48:05,050
Don't call me that, please.

363
00:48:05,350 --> 00:48:09,350
- What are you doing here?  - I came to get you...

364
00:48:18,650 --> 00:48:23,650
Late 14th century, Ming Dynasty. It breaks the heart.

365
00:48:23,950 --> 00:48:26,050
And the head!  You hit me.

366
00:48:26,350 --> 00:48:28,850
I'll never forgive myself.

367
00:48:29,050 --> 00:48:31,450
Don't worry, I'm fine.

368
00:48:32,950 --> 00:48:34,950
Thank God.

369
00:48:37,150 --> 00:48:40,050
It's a fake!

370
00:48:40,250 --> 00:48:43,350
You can tell by the cross-section.

371
00:48:44,650 --> 00:48:45,950
No!

372
00:48:46,150 --> 00:48:48,350
We've got to get out of here.

373
00:48:48,550 --> 00:48:52,750
Sorry about your head. I thought you were one of them.

374
00:48:52,950 --> 00:48:56,150
Dad, they come in through the doors!

375
00:48:56,450 --> 00:48:59,750
Good point. But better safe than sorry.

376
00:48:59,950 --> 00:49:05,350
I was wrong this time, but not when I mailed you my diary.

377
00:49:05,550 --> 00:49:07,950
You obviously got it.

378
00:49:09,450 --> 00:49:14,550
I got it and I used it. We found the entrance to the catacombs.

379
00:49:14,750 --> 00:49:17,950
- Through the library?  - Right.

380
00:49:21,550 --> 00:49:23,250
I knew it.

381
00:49:23,450 --> 00:49:26,250
And the tomb of Sir Richard?

382
00:49:26,450 --> 00:49:28,950
Found it.

383
00:49:29,250 --> 00:49:31,650
He was actually there?

384
00:49:33,550 --> 00:49:37,750
- You saw him?  - What was left of him.

385
00:49:37,950 --> 00:49:40,250
And his shield?

386
00:49:40,450 --> 00:49:44,350
The inscription on Sir Richard's shield?

387
00:49:44,550 --> 00:49:49,450
- Alexandretta.  - Alexandretta!  Of course!

388
00:49:49,650 --> 00:49:53,450
On the pilgrim trail from the Eastern Empire.

389
00:49:55,850 --> 00:49:58,050
Junior, you did it!

390
00:49:58,350 --> 00:50:01,450
No, Dad, you did. Forty years.

391
00:50:02,850 --> 00:50:07,550
- If only I could have been with you.  - There were rats, Dad.

392
00:50:07,850 --> 00:50:09,850
- Rats?  - Yeah. Big ones.

393
00:50:10,550 --> 00:50:14,650
- What do the Nazis want with you?  - They wanted my diary.

394
00:50:14,850 --> 00:50:20,750
I had to get that book as far away from me as I could.

395
00:50:23,050 --> 00:50:24,090
Yeah

396
00:50:27,950 --> 00:50:29,450
Dr Jones.

397
00:50:29,650 --> 00:50:31,950
- Yes?  - Yes?

398
00:50:33,850 --> 00:50:36,850
I will take the book now.

399
00:50:38,050 --> 00:50:42,950
- What book?  - You have the diary in your pocket.

400
00:50:43,150 --> 00:50:44,650
You dolt!

401
00:50:44,850 --> 00:50:50,950
You think my son would be that stupid, bringing my diary back here?

402
00:50:52,350 --> 00:50:54,750
You didn't, did you?

403
00:50:55,050 --> 00:50:57,050
You didn't bring it, did you?

404
00:50:57,350 --> 00:51:01,050
- You did!  - Can we discuss this later?

405
00:51:01,350 --> 00:51:04,750
- I mailed it to the Marx Brothers!  - Take it easy!

406
00:51:05,050 --> 00:51:10,050
Take it easy?  I sent it home so it wouldn't fall into their hands!

407
00:51:10,250 --> 00:51:13,250
- I came here to save you!  - Oh, yeah?

408
00:51:13,450 --> 00:51:17,050
- Who's going to save you, Junior?  - I told you...

409
00:51:24,650 --> 00:51:26,550
...don't call me Junior.

410
00:51:29,250 --> 00:51:31,650
Look what you did!

411
00:51:32,850 --> 00:51:34,950
I can't believe what you...

412
00:51:37,250 --> 00:51:38,850
Elsa?

413
00:51:39,050 --> 00:51:43,250
That's far enough. Put down the gun or the Frlein dies.

414
00:51:43,550 --> 00:51:46,850
She's one of them. She's a Nazi.

415
00:51:47,050 --> 00:51:49,050
- What?  - Trust me.

416
00:51:49,250 --> 00:51:52,250
- I will kill her.  - Go ahead.

417
00:51:52,450 --> 00:51:55,750
- Don't shoot!  - Don't worry, he won't.

418
00:51:55,950 --> 00:51:59,750
- Indy, please, do what he says!  - Don't listen to her.

419
00:51:59,950 --> 00:52:01,550
Enough. She dies!

420
00:52:01,950 --> 00:52:03,450
Wait!

421
00:52:10,750 --> 00:52:12,350
I'm sorry.

422
00:52:12,650 --> 00:52:15,450
No. Don't be.

423
00:52:28,450 --> 00:52:32,650
But you should have listened to your father.

424
00:52:49,550 --> 00:52:53,750
She ransacked her own room, and I fell for it.

425
00:52:54,950 --> 00:52:58,150
How did you know she was a Nazi?

426
00:52:58,350 --> 00:53:00,850
How did you know she was a Nazi?

427
00:53:01,050 --> 00:53:03,850
She talks in her sleep.

428
00:53:13,150 --> 00:53:15,250
I didn't trust her. Why did you?

429
00:53:15,450 --> 00:53:18,850
Because he didn't take my advice.

430
00:53:25,150 --> 00:53:26,650
Donovan.

431
00:53:26,850 --> 00:53:30,550
Didn't I warn you not to trust anybody, Dr Jones?

432
00:53:30,750 --> 00:53:33,150
I misjudged you, Walter.

433
00:53:33,450 --> 00:53:36,250
I knew you'd sell your mother,

434
00:53:36,450 --> 00:53:42,250
but not your country and your soul to the slime of humanity.

435
00:53:43,850 --> 00:53:47,950
Dr Schneider, there are pages torn out of this.

436
00:53:56,950 --> 00:54:00,650
This book contained a map, a map with no names.

437
00:54:00,850 --> 00:54:05,050
Directions from the unknown city to the secret canyon.

438
00:54:05,250 --> 00:54:09,550
- So it did.  - Where are these missing pages?

439
00:54:09,750 --> 00:54:15,050
You're wasting your breath. He won't tell us. He doesn't have to.

440
00:54:15,250 --> 00:54:20,850
It's obvious where the pages are. He's given them to Marcus Brody.

441
00:54:21,150 --> 00:54:25,750
Marcus?  You dragged him along?  He's not up to the challenge.

442
00:54:26,050 --> 00:54:28,650
He sticks out. We'll find him.

443
00:54:28,850 --> 00:54:33,550
The hell you will. He's got a two-day head start on you.

444
00:54:34,350 --> 00:54:39,750
Brody's got friends all over, he speaks a dozen languages,

445
00:54:40,050 --> 00:54:43,750
he'll blend in, disappear. You'll never see him again.

446
00:54:44,050 --> 00:54:47,150
With any luck, he's got the Grail already.

447
00:54:47,850 --> 00:54:50,850
Does anyone speak English? Ancient Greek?

448
00:54:51,150 --> 00:54:53,450
Water?  No, fish make love in it.

449
00:54:55,260 --> 00:55:00,400
Thank you so much. No, I really don't want... Thank you.

450
00:55:00,550 --> 00:55:02,250
No, I'm a vegetarian.

451
00:55:02,520 --> 00:55:05,640
Does anyone understand a word I'm saying?

452
00:55:05,750 --> 00:55:08,450
- Mr Brody!  - What a relief.

453
00:55:08,650 --> 00:55:10,850
Marcus Brody, sir!

454
00:55:11,150 --> 00:55:14,250
- Where's Indy?  - In Austria. A slight detour.

455
00:55:14,450 --> 00:55:18,150
- You're alone?  - Yes, but everything's under control.

456
00:55:18,350 --> 00:55:22,650
- You've arranged supplies?  - Yes. But where are we going?

457
00:55:22,850 --> 00:55:26,350
This map will show you. It was drawn by...

458
00:55:26,550 --> 00:55:29,750
Mr Brody. Welcome to Iskenderun.

459
00:55:30,050 --> 00:55:33,050
The museum director has sent a car for you.

460
00:55:33,250 --> 00:55:36,750
- Well... Your servant, sir.  - And I am his.

461
00:55:37,650 --> 00:55:38,850
Follow me, please.

462
00:55:39,050 --> 00:55:43,050
- My reputation precedes me.  - There is no museum.

463
00:55:43,350 --> 00:55:47,050
- Papers, please!  - Papers?  Of course.

464
00:55:47,250 --> 00:55:48,650
- Run. - Yes.

465
00:55:48,850 --> 00:55:52,150
Papers?  Got it here. Just finished reading it.

466
00:55:52,450 --> 00:55:54,350
- Run. - Yes.

467
00:55:54,550 --> 00:55:57,650
- Egyptian Mail, morning edition. Run!  - Did you say...?

468
00:55:57,850 --> 00:55:59,950
Run!

469
00:56:14,350 --> 00:56:18,450
Up here, up here, quick!  Find the back door!

470
00:56:32,350 --> 00:56:34,350
Intolerable.

471
00:56:36,750 --> 00:56:40,150
Dr Schneider, you must return to Berlin at once.

472
00:56:40,450 --> 00:56:43,150
A rally at the Institute of Aryan Culture.

473
00:56:43,350 --> 00:56:47,350
Your presence is requested at the highest level.

474
00:56:47,550 --> 00:56:50,650
Thank you. I'll meet you at Iskenderun.

475
00:56:50,850 --> 00:56:55,250
Take this diary to the Reichsmuseum and show them our progress.

476
00:56:55,450 --> 00:56:57,550
Without the map, it's useless.

477
00:56:57,850 --> 00:57:00,050
- Let me kill them now. - No.

478
00:57:00,250 --> 00:57:03,750
If we don't find the pages, we'll need them alive.

479
00:57:04,050 --> 00:57:07,050
Always do what the doctor orders.

480
00:57:14,350 --> 00:57:18,350
Don't look at me like that. We both wanted the Grail.

481
00:57:18,550 --> 00:57:22,350
I would have done anything to get it. You would have, too.

482
00:57:22,650 --> 00:57:24,850
I'm sorry you think so.

483
00:57:35,850 --> 00:57:39,250
I can't forget how wonderful it was.

484
00:57:39,550 --> 00:57:42,850
Thank you. It was rather wonderful.

485
00:57:51,550 --> 00:57:56,550
Dr Schneider. Your car is waiting.

486
00:58:04,650 --> 00:58:08,450
That's how Austrians say goodbye.

487
00:58:13,150 --> 00:58:17,650
And this is how we say goodbye in Germany, Dr Jones.

488
00:58:25,350 --> 00:58:29,250
- I like the Austrian way better. - So did I.

489
00:58:29,450 --> 00:58:33,650
Get the ropes loose. We gotta get to Marcus before the Nazis.

490
00:58:33,850 --> 00:58:37,450
You said he had two days' start, that he'd blend in.

491
00:58:37,850 --> 00:58:43,750
Are you kidding?  I made that up. He got lost once in his own museum!

492
00:58:45,050 --> 00:58:48,450
Can you reach my left jacket pocket?

493
00:58:48,650 --> 00:58:53,050
- What am I looking for?  - My lucky charm.

494
00:58:53,250 --> 00:58:55,950
Feels like a cigarette lighter.

495
00:58:56,150 --> 00:58:58,450
Burn through the ropes.

496
00:58:59,050 --> 00:59:00,450
Very good.

497
00:59:25,250 --> 00:59:29,450
- I ought to tell you something.  - Don't get sentimental now.

498
00:59:29,650 --> 00:59:32,650
The floor's on fire. See?

499
00:59:33,450 --> 00:59:36,850
- And the chair.  - Move!  Move!

500
00:59:37,050 --> 00:59:39,950
- Approaching the table.  - Move!

501
00:59:54,450 --> 00:59:56,550
We have Marcus Brody.

502
00:59:56,750 --> 00:59:59,550
And more important, we have the map!

503
01:00:03,550 --> 01:00:07,050
"By command of the Frer. Secrecy essential to success.

504
01:00:07,250 --> 01:00:10,150
"Eliminate the American conspirators."

505
01:00:10,450 --> 01:00:13,750
Germany has declared war on the Jones boys!

506
01:00:19,350 --> 01:00:21,450
- Dad?  - What?

507
01:00:21,750 --> 01:00:23,050
- Dad?  - What?

508
01:00:23,350 --> 01:00:25,650
Head for the fireplace!

509
01:00:37,950 --> 01:00:41,650
I think I can get these ropes off. Whoops!

510
01:00:53,350 --> 01:00:57,450
- Our situation has not improved.  - Listen, I'm almost free.

511
01:01:23,550 --> 01:01:26,150
This is intolerable!

512
01:01:26,550 --> 01:01:29,250
- I'm out, Dad.  - Well done, boy!

513
01:02:05,450 --> 01:02:07,950
Come on, Dad.

514
01:02:13,250 --> 01:02:14,950
Dead end.

515
01:02:15,350 --> 01:02:20,350
There's got to be a secret door or... a passageway.

516
01:02:20,550 --> 01:02:23,650
I find that if I just sit down and think...

517
01:02:32,350 --> 01:02:35,350
...the solution presents itself.

518
01:02:42,650 --> 01:02:45,150
Great!  More boats!

519
01:02:48,050 --> 01:02:52,950
This has been just another typical day for you, eh?

520
01:02:53,250 --> 01:02:56,850
No!  It's been better than most.

521
01:02:58,650 --> 01:03:00,550
Come on, Dad!

522
01:03:00,750 --> 01:03:03,650
What?  We're not going on the boat?

523
01:05:37,050 --> 01:05:39,550
Stop!  Stop!

524
01:05:40,850 --> 01:05:44,250
You're going the wrong way. We have to get to Berlin.

525
01:05:44,450 --> 01:05:47,450
- Brody's this way. - My diary's in Berlin.

526
01:05:47,850 --> 01:05:51,150
We don't need the diary, Marcus has the map.

527
01:05:51,350 --> 01:05:55,250
There is more in the diary than just the map.

528
01:05:55,450 --> 01:05:57,650
All right, Dad. Tell me.

529
01:05:59,250 --> 01:06:03,750
Well, he who finds the Grail must face the final challenge.

530
01:06:03,950 --> 01:06:08,450
- What final challenge?  - Three devices of lethal cunning.

531
01:06:08,650 --> 01:06:11,550
- Booby traps?  - Oh, yes!

532
01:06:11,750 --> 01:06:15,150
But I found the clues to safely take us through

533
01:06:15,350 --> 01:06:17,450
in the Chronicles of St Anselm!

534
01:06:17,650 --> 01:06:20,550
Well, what are they?

535
01:06:22,750 --> 01:06:25,150
Can't you remember?

536
01:06:25,950 --> 01:06:29,950
I wrote them in my diary so I wouldn't have to remember!

537
01:06:30,150 --> 01:06:34,950
Half the German army's after us and you want to go to Berlin?!

538
01:06:35,150 --> 01:06:37,850
Yes. All that matters is the Grail.

539
01:06:38,150 --> 01:06:42,050
- What about Marcus?  - Marcus would agree with me.

540
01:06:42,250 --> 01:06:45,550
Two selfless martyrs. Jesus Christ!

541
01:06:49,250 --> 01:06:52,050
That's for blasphemy.

542
01:06:54,550 --> 01:06:59,450
The quest for the Grail is not archaeology.

543
01:06:59,650 --> 01:07:02,050
It's a race against evil.

544
01:07:02,250 --> 01:07:04,550
If it is captured by the Nazis,

545
01:07:04,850 --> 01:07:08,450
the armies of darkness will march all over the earth.

546
01:07:08,750 --> 01:07:11,150
Do you understand me?

547
01:07:11,450 --> 01:07:16,150
This is an obsession, Dad. I never understood it.

548
01:07:16,350 --> 01:07:18,050
Never.

549
01:07:19,150 --> 01:07:21,050
And neither did Mom.

550
01:07:21,250 --> 01:07:25,050
Oh, yes, she did. Only too well...

551
01:07:26,450 --> 01:07:27,850
...unfortunately.

552
01:07:28,250 --> 01:07:34,150
She kept her illness from me. All I could do was mourn her.

553
01:08:35,450 --> 01:08:38,850
My boy, we're pilgrims in an unholy land.

554
01:09:08,550 --> 01:09:11,850
Frlein Doctor. Where is it?

555
01:09:12,150 --> 01:09:16,350
- How did you get here?  - Where is it?  I want it.

556
01:09:18,250 --> 01:09:23,350
- You came back for the book? Why?  - My father didn't want it incinerated.

557
01:09:23,650 --> 01:09:27,450
Is that what you think of me?  I believe in the Grail, not the swastika.

558
01:09:27,850 --> 01:09:33,050
You count as the enemy of the Grail. Who cares what you think?

559
01:09:33,250 --> 01:09:35,150
You do!

560
01:09:35,350 --> 01:09:38,450
All I have to do is squeeze.

561
01:09:38,750 --> 01:09:41,750
All I have to do is scream.

562
01:09:50,550 --> 01:09:53,650
Got it. Let's get the hell outta here.

563
01:11:06,550 --> 01:11:11,650
- What did you get?   - First available flight out of Germany.

564
01:11:36,850 --> 01:11:39,250
Well, we made it.

565
01:11:40,450 --> 01:11:43,150
When we're airborne, with Germany behind us,

566
01:11:43,350 --> 01:11:45,650
then I'll share that sentiment.

567
01:11:45,850 --> 01:11:47,850
Relax.

568
01:12:33,250 --> 01:12:35,050
Tickets, please.

569
01:12:54,050 --> 01:12:55,650
Tickets, please.

570
01:13:10,950 --> 01:13:12,350
No ticket!

571
01:13:16,750 --> 01:13:18,750
- Ticket!  - Ticket!

572
01:13:36,450 --> 01:13:41,950
You know, sharing your adventures is an interesting experience.

573
01:13:43,250 --> 01:13:45,250
It's not all we shared.

574
01:13:45,450 --> 01:13:49,450
It's disgraceful. You're old enough to be her... grandfather!

575
01:13:49,650 --> 01:13:54,350
- I'm as human as the next man.  - I was the next man.

576
01:13:55,950 --> 01:13:59,450
Of course... Ships that pass in the night.

577
01:13:59,750 --> 01:14:03,750
Remember the last time we had a quiet drink?

578
01:14:05,150 --> 01:14:07,950
I had a milkshake.

579
01:14:08,150 --> 01:14:10,950
What did we talk about?

580
01:14:11,350 --> 01:14:13,750
We didn't talk.

581
01:14:13,950 --> 01:14:16,450
We never talked.

582
01:14:17,650 --> 01:14:20,050
Do I detect a rebuke?

583
01:14:21,550 --> 01:14:26,650
A regret. It was just the two of us, it was a lonely way to grow up.

584
01:14:26,950 --> 01:14:31,650
If you'd been an ordinary father, you'd have understood that.

585
01:14:31,850 --> 01:14:36,250
- Actually, I was a wonderful father.  - When?

586
01:14:36,450 --> 01:14:40,650
Did I ever tell you to eat up, go to bed, do your homework?  No.

587
01:14:40,950 --> 01:14:45,250
I respected your privacy, and I taught you self-reliance!

588
01:14:45,550 --> 01:14:48,450
You taught me I was less important than people

589
01:14:48,750 --> 01:14:51,350
who'd been dead for 500 years!

590
01:14:51,550 --> 01:14:55,750
I learned it so well, we've hardly spoken for 20 years!

591
01:14:56,050 --> 01:14:59,550
You left just when you were becoming interesting!

592
01:14:59,750 --> 01:15:05,050
- Dad, how can...?  - Very well. I'm here now.

593
01:15:05,350 --> 01:15:08,350
What do you want to talk about?

594
01:15:18,350 --> 01:15:23,550
- I can't think of anything.  - Then why are you complaining?

595
01:15:23,750 --> 01:15:26,250
We have work to do.

596
01:15:27,550 --> 01:15:33,050
When we get to Alexandretta, we will face three challenges.

597
01:15:33,250 --> 01:15:35,250
First: "The Breath of God.

598
01:15:35,450 --> 01:15:37,850
"Only the penitent man will pass."

599
01:15:38,050 --> 01:15:44,050
Second: "The Word of God. Only in the footsteps of God will he proceed."

600
01:15:44,250 --> 01:15:46,650
Third: "The Path of God.

601
01:15:46,850 --> 01:15:52,350
"Only in the leap from the lion's head will he prove his worth."

602
01:15:53,850 --> 01:15:55,850
What does that mean?

603
01:15:56,150 --> 01:15:59,050
I don't know. We'll find out!

604
01:16:06,550 --> 01:16:10,950
We're turning around. They're taking us back to Germany.

605
01:16:18,050 --> 01:16:23,650
I thought it'd take them longer to figure out the radio was dead. Move!

606
01:16:27,150 --> 01:16:29,950
Come on, Dad! Come on!

607
01:16:42,950 --> 01:16:47,150
- I didn't know you could fly a plane.  - Fly, yes...

608
01:16:48,450 --> 01:16:50,650
...land, no.

609
01:17:14,750 --> 01:17:18,550
You're gonna have to use the machine gun. Get it ready!

610
01:17:19,750 --> 01:17:23,350
Eleven o'clock! Dad, eleven o'clock!

611
01:17:25,250 --> 01:17:27,350
What happens at eleven o'clock?

612
01:17:27,650 --> 01:17:31,250
Twelve, eleven, ten... Eleven o'clock, fire!

613
01:17:57,550 --> 01:17:59,950
Dad, are we hit?

614
01:18:00,250 --> 01:18:02,350
More or less.

615
01:18:02,650 --> 01:18:05,850
Son, I'm sorry. They got us.

616
01:18:08,250 --> 01:18:11,250
Hang on, Dad, we're going in!

617
01:18:23,250 --> 01:18:25,450
- Nice landing.  - Thanks.

618
01:18:41,050 --> 01:18:44,950
- Those people are trying to kill us.  - I know, Dad!

619
01:18:45,150 --> 01:18:49,250
- It's a new experience for me.  - It happens to me all the time.

620
01:19:20,150 --> 01:19:22,950
This is intolerable!

621
01:19:23,250 --> 01:19:25,650
This could be close.

622
01:19:33,650 --> 01:19:36,050
Faster, boy, faster!

623
01:19:51,150 --> 01:19:54,050
They don't come any closer than that!

624
01:20:09,450 --> 01:20:10,750
Dad!

625
01:20:11,050 --> 01:20:13,150
He's coming back.

626
01:20:59,050 --> 01:21:01,750
I suddenly remembered my Charlemagne.

627
01:21:02,050 --> 01:21:08,150
"Let my armies be the rocks and the trees and the birds in the sky."

628
01:21:24,550 --> 01:21:28,050
These pages are taken from Professor Jones'diary,

629
01:21:28,250 --> 01:21:32,450
and they include a map pinpointing the location of the Grail.

630
01:21:32,750 --> 01:21:35,350
The Grail is all but in our hands.

631
01:21:35,650 --> 01:21:39,750
However, we would not cross your soil without permission,

632
01:21:39,950 --> 01:21:45,450
nor remove the Grail from your borders without compensation.

633
01:21:47,650 --> 01:21:50,350
What have you brought?

634
01:21:57,350 --> 01:22:00,050
Precious valuables, your Highness,

635
01:22:00,250 --> 01:22:04,550
donated by some of the finest families in all of Germany...

636
01:22:09,350 --> 01:22:12,050
Rolls-Royce Phantom II...

637
01:22:12,250 --> 01:22:16,350
4.3-litre, 30-horsepower, 6-cylinder engine,

638
01:22:16,650 --> 01:22:19,250
with Stromberg down-draught carburettor.

639
01:22:19,450 --> 01:22:25,450
Can go from 0-100 kph in 12.5 seconds.

640
01:22:25,750 --> 01:22:27,950
And I even like the colour.

641
01:22:28,150 --> 01:22:31,850
The keys are in the ignition, your Highness.

642
01:22:32,150 --> 01:22:36,350
You shall have camels, horses, an armed escort,

643
01:22:36,550 --> 01:22:40,150
provisions, desert vehicles and tanks.

644
01:22:40,450 --> 01:22:42,450
You're welcome.

645
01:22:57,850 --> 01:23:01,550
No time to lose. Indiana Jones and his father have escaped.

646
01:23:04,650 --> 01:23:08,850
We go this way. Get that camel out of the way.

647
01:23:10,350 --> 01:23:12,550
What happened to Marcus?

648
01:23:12,750 --> 01:23:18,650
They set out across the desert this afternoon. They took him with them.

649
01:23:19,650 --> 01:23:22,050
Now they have the map!

650
01:23:23,650 --> 01:23:27,950
In this race, there's no silver medal for finishing second.

651
01:23:50,550 --> 01:23:54,750
- Care to wet your whistle, Marcus?  - I'd rather spit in your face.

652
01:23:55,050 --> 01:23:58,150
But as I haven't got any spit...

653
01:23:59,850 --> 01:24:05,950
Must be within three or four miles. Otherwise we're off the map.

654
01:24:06,950 --> 01:24:10,350
Marcus, we're on the brink of the recovery

655
01:24:10,650 --> 01:24:14,150
of the greatest artefact in the history of mankind.

656
01:24:14,450 --> 01:24:19,550
You're meddling with powers you cannot possibly comprehend.

657
01:24:25,350 --> 01:24:28,850
I see Brody. He seems OK.

658
01:24:32,250 --> 01:24:34,650
They've got a tank.

659
01:24:40,050 --> 01:24:41,950
A six-pound gun.

660
01:24:42,250 --> 01:24:47,250
- What are you doing?  Get down!  - Dad, we're well out of range.

661
01:24:57,050 --> 01:24:59,850
- That car was my brother-in-law's.  - Come on!

662
01:25:00,150 --> 01:25:03,450
- I can't see anyone up there.  - Maybe it wasn't Jones.

663
01:25:03,750 --> 01:25:07,850
No. It was him, all right. He's here somewhere.

664
01:25:08,850 --> 01:25:11,450
Put Brody in the tank.

665
01:25:17,650 --> 01:25:22,750
In this sun, without transportation, they're as good as dead.

666
01:25:32,050 --> 01:25:33,650
It's Jones, all right.

667
01:25:38,250 --> 01:25:39,550
Who are they?

668
01:25:39,750 --> 01:25:43,250
Who cares, as long as they keep Donovan busy?

669
01:25:43,550 --> 01:25:48,450
Dad, stay here while we organise some transportation.

670
01:26:11,050 --> 01:26:13,650
- I'll get the horses.  - I'll take the camels.

671
01:26:13,850 --> 01:26:16,850
I don't need camels. No camels!

672
01:26:28,250 --> 01:26:30,650
Who is he?

673
01:26:30,850 --> 01:26:33,650
A messenger from God.

674
01:26:34,750 --> 01:26:40,850
For the unrighteous, the Cup of Life holds everlasting damnation.

675
01:26:59,650 --> 01:27:01,050
Marcus.

676
01:27:02,550 --> 01:27:05,450
"That genius of the Restoration..."

677
01:27:05,850 --> 01:27:08,050
"...aid our own resuscitation."

678
01:27:08,450 --> 01:27:12,350
- Henry!  What are you doing here?  - It's a rescue.

679
01:27:17,950 --> 01:27:19,950
Search him.

680
01:27:20,150 --> 01:27:25,650
What is this book, that miserable little diary of yours?

681
01:27:25,850 --> 01:27:32,250
The book is useless, yet you come back to Berlin to get it. Why?

682
01:27:32,450 --> 01:27:34,550
What are you hiding?

683
01:27:34,850 --> 01:27:38,950
What does the diary tell you that it doesn't tell us?

684
01:27:39,250 --> 01:27:43,350
It tells me that goose-stepping morons like yourself

685
01:27:43,550 --> 01:27:46,550
should try reading books instead of burning them!

686
01:27:47,150 --> 01:27:50,150
Colonel! Jones is getting away!

687
01:27:50,350 --> 01:27:52,850
I think not, Herr Donovan.

688
01:27:53,050 --> 01:27:56,450
Not that Jones, the other Jones!

689
01:28:05,150 --> 01:28:09,550
Sallah, I said no camels, that's five camels!  Can't you count?

690
01:28:09,850 --> 01:28:14,050
Compensation for the car. Your father and Brody...

691
01:28:14,350 --> 01:28:19,250
- Where's my father?  - In the belly of that steel beast.

692
01:30:44,450 --> 01:30:45,950
Dad!

693
01:30:46,150 --> 01:30:47,550
Dad!  Dad!

694
01:30:48,250 --> 01:30:50,450
Junior! Junior!

695
01:30:50,850 --> 01:30:52,150
Junior!

696
01:32:07,450 --> 01:32:08,750
Dad!

697
01:32:10,050 --> 01:32:12,750
Dad!  Get out!

698
01:33:33,150 --> 01:33:36,150
- Henry, the pen.  - What?

699
01:33:36,350 --> 01:33:39,450
The pen is mightier than the sword.

700
01:34:01,950 --> 01:34:05,050
- Look what you did!  - It's war!

701
01:34:10,750 --> 01:34:13,550
Didn't I tell you it was a rescue?

702
01:35:17,650 --> 01:35:19,050
Dad!

703
01:35:20,350 --> 01:35:24,450
- You call this archaeology?  - Get out of there!

704
01:35:34,550 --> 01:35:37,650
How does one get off this thing?

705
01:35:43,450 --> 01:35:45,050
Where's Marcus?

706
01:35:54,050 --> 01:35:56,550
Hang on, Dad!

707
01:36:02,050 --> 01:36:07,250
- Father of Indy, give me your hand!  - Sallah, get Dad!

708
01:36:07,450 --> 01:36:09,250
Give me your hand!

709
01:36:58,050 --> 01:36:59,650
Junior!

710
01:37:01,750 --> 01:37:03,350
Indy!

711
01:37:12,350 --> 01:37:14,450
Oh, God.

712
01:37:14,650 --> 01:37:16,550
I've lost him.

713
01:37:20,250 --> 01:37:23,250
And I never told him anything.

714
01:37:25,350 --> 01:37:28,650
I just wasn't ready, Marcus.

715
01:37:28,850 --> 01:37:32,650
Five minutes would have been enough.

716
01:38:08,450 --> 01:38:11,350
I thought I'd lost you, boy!

717
01:38:12,650 --> 01:38:15,750
I thought you had, too, sir.

718
01:38:27,950 --> 01:38:29,250
Well...

719
01:38:31,950 --> 01:38:34,550
Well done. Come on.

720
01:38:37,950 --> 01:38:39,950
Let's go, then!

721
01:38:41,950 --> 01:38:47,650
Why are you sitting there resting when we're so near the end?  Let's go!

722
01:38:59,750 --> 01:39:03,650
The Canyon of the Crescent Moon.

723
01:41:30,450 --> 01:41:32,050
Helmut, another volunteer!

724
01:42:01,350 --> 01:42:06,450
- I never expected to see you again.  - Like a bad penny, I always turn up.

725
01:42:06,750 --> 01:42:10,950
Step back now, Dr Schneider. Give Dr Jones some room.

726
01:42:11,150 --> 01:42:14,150
He's going to recover the Grail for us.

727
01:42:16,450 --> 01:42:20,550
Impossible?  What do you say?  Ready to go down in history?

728
01:42:20,750 --> 01:42:23,650
As what?  A Nazi stooge, like you?

729
01:42:23,850 --> 01:42:27,850
The Nazis? Is that the limit of your vision?

730
01:42:28,150 --> 01:42:31,750
The Nazis want to write themselves into the Grail legend,

731
01:42:32,050 --> 01:42:34,050
take on the world.

732
01:42:34,450 --> 01:42:36,950
They're welcome.

733
01:42:37,250 --> 01:42:39,350
But I want the Grail itself.

734
01:42:39,650 --> 01:42:43,350
The cup that gives everlasting life.

735
01:42:43,550 --> 01:42:46,950
Hitler can have the world, but he can't take it with him.

736
01:42:47,150 --> 01:42:51,150
I'll be drinking my own health when he's gone the way of the dodo.

737
01:42:55,050 --> 01:42:59,250
The Grail is mine. And you're going to get it for me.

738
01:42:59,550 --> 01:43:01,850
Shooting me won't get you anywhere.

739
01:43:02,050 --> 01:43:05,650
You know something?  You're absolutely right.

740
01:43:08,750 --> 01:43:10,350
Dad!

741
01:43:10,550 --> 01:43:13,550
- Dad!  - Junior.

742
01:43:13,750 --> 01:43:15,350
- No. - Get back!

743
01:43:33,350 --> 01:43:39,050
You can't save him when you're dead. The Grail is all that can save him.

744
01:43:39,350 --> 01:43:42,650
It's time to ask yourself what you believe.

745
01:44:18,450 --> 01:44:20,450
"The Breath of God.

746
01:44:21,550 --> 01:44:24,450
Only the penitent man will pass."

747
01:44:25,250 --> 01:44:27,950
The penitent man will pass.

748
01:44:46,050 --> 01:44:49,150
Penitent man will pass.

749
01:44:49,450 --> 01:44:51,450
Penitent man...

750
01:44:53,950 --> 01:44:56,650
"Only the penitent man will pass."

751
01:44:56,850 --> 01:44:59,050
"Only the penitent man will pass."

752
01:44:59,250 --> 01:45:04,650
The penitent man will pass. Penitent? Penitent man?

753
01:45:04,850 --> 01:45:07,250
Penitent man... penitent...

754
01:45:07,450 --> 01:45:10,350
The penitent man is humble before God.

755
01:45:10,550 --> 01:45:12,150
Penitent... penitent...

756
01:45:12,450 --> 01:45:14,050
Penitent man...?

757
01:45:14,350 --> 01:45:17,350
The penitent man is humble...

758
01:45:17,550 --> 01:45:19,550
...kneels before God. Kneel!

759
01:45:28,950 --> 01:45:30,150
I'm through!

760
01:45:32,450 --> 01:45:33,850
We're through.

761
01:45:36,150 --> 01:45:39,050
- He's all right!  - No...

762
01:45:39,350 --> 01:45:42,650
The second challenge is the Word of God.

763
01:45:42,850 --> 01:45:46,850
"Only in the footsteps of God will he proceed."

764
01:45:50,150 --> 01:45:52,050
The Word of God?

765
01:45:52,450 --> 01:45:53,650
Word of...?

766
01:45:56,750 --> 01:46:00,350
Proceed in the footsteps of the Word...

767
01:46:00,550 --> 01:46:05,550
- The Word of God!  - No, Henry, try not to talk.

768
01:46:10,850 --> 01:46:13,150
The name of God.

769
01:46:14,650 --> 01:46:17,050
The name of God.

770
01:46:18,850 --> 01:46:21,050
Jehovah.

771
01:46:21,350 --> 01:46:26,750
But in the Latin alphabet, Jehovah begins with an "l".

772
01:46:27,050 --> 01:46:28,450
J...

773
01:46:34,750 --> 01:46:36,750
Oh, dear.

774
01:46:40,250 --> 01:46:41,750
Idiot!

775
01:46:42,050 --> 01:46:45,250
In Latin, Jehovah starts with an "l".

776
01:46:47,550 --> 01:46:48,950
I...

777
01:46:49,950 --> 01:46:51,550
E...

778
01:47:21,850 --> 01:47:23,750
The Path of God.

779
01:47:27,450 --> 01:47:33,050
"Only in the leap from the lion's head will he prove his worth."

780
01:47:35,850 --> 01:47:40,450
lmpossible. Nobody can jump this.

781
01:47:42,550 --> 01:47:45,450
Indy!  You must hurry!

782
01:47:45,650 --> 01:47:47,650
Come quickly!

783
01:47:53,250 --> 01:47:55,250
It's a leap of faith.

784
01:47:57,450 --> 01:48:02,250
You must believe, boy!  You must... believe.

785
01:49:49,050 --> 01:49:51,150
I knew you'd come.

786
01:49:51,450 --> 01:49:53,750
But my strength has left me.

787
01:49:54,750 --> 01:49:55,750
Who are you?

788
01:49:55,950 --> 01:49:59,950
The last of three brothers who swore an oath

789
01:49:59,950 --> 01:50:04,150
to find the Grail and to guard it.

790
01:50:04,450 --> 01:50:06,950
That was seven hundred years ago.

791
01:50:06,950 --> 01:50:09,950
A long time to wait.

792
01:50:11,150 --> 01:50:14,350
You're strangely dressed for a knight.

793
01:50:19,750 --> 01:50:25,350
I was chosen because I was the bravest and most worthy.

794
01:50:25,650 --> 01:50:28,950
The honour was mine until another came

795
01:50:29,150 --> 01:50:32,850
to challenge me to single combat.

796
01:50:33,050 --> 01:50:36,650
I pass it to you...

797
01:50:37,650 --> 01:50:40,050
...who vanquished me.

798
01:50:42,650 --> 01:50:45,050
I don't have time to explain, but...

799
01:51:02,250 --> 01:51:03,750
Which one is it?

800
01:51:04,050 --> 01:51:06,050
You must choose.

801
01:51:06,250 --> 01:51:08,350
But choose wisely.

802
01:51:08,650 --> 01:51:11,950
For as the true Grail will bring you life,

803
01:51:12,150 --> 01:51:15,850
the false Grail will take it from you.

804
01:51:21,550 --> 01:51:23,250
I'm not a historian.

805
01:51:23,260 --> 01:51:25,250
I have no idea what it looks like.

806
01:51:25,250 --> 01:51:26,050
Which one is it?

807
01:51:27,950 --> 01:51:30,250
Let me choose.

808
01:51:30,450 --> 01:51:32,750
Thank you, Doctor.

809
01:51:45,450 --> 01:51:47,350
Oh, yes.

810
01:51:53,050 --> 01:51:56,150
It's more beautiful than I ever imagined.

811
01:52:07,550 --> 01:52:11,750
This certainly is the Cup of the King of Kings.

812
01:52:17,450 --> 01:52:19,450
Eternal life.

813
01:52:54,450 --> 01:52:57,350
What is happening to me?

814
01:53:01,350 --> 01:53:04,150
What is happening?

815
01:53:26,350 --> 01:53:28,550
He chose...

816
01:53:28,850 --> 01:53:30,650
...poorly.

817
01:53:40,450 --> 01:53:42,250
It would not be made out of gold.

818
01:53:42,650 --> 01:53:45,650
That's the cup of a carpenter.

819
01:53:55,250 --> 01:53:58,550
There's only one way to find out.

820
01:54:11,850 --> 01:54:15,250
You have chosen wisely.

821
01:54:16,650 --> 01:54:20,850
But the Grail cannot pass beyond the Great Seal.

822
01:54:21,050 --> 01:54:26,850
That is the boundary and the price of immortality.

823
01:55:52,550 --> 01:55:55,350
Drop your guns... please!

824
01:55:58,750 --> 01:56:01,550
Dad, get to your feet.

825
01:56:15,150 --> 01:56:18,350
We have got it. Come on!

826
01:56:18,650 --> 01:56:20,950
Elsa, don't move!

827
01:56:21,150 --> 01:56:25,650
- It's ours, Indy. Yours and mine.  - Don't cross the seal.

828
01:56:25,950 --> 01:56:31,150
The knight warned us not to take the Grail from here.

829
01:56:55,350 --> 01:56:57,850
Junior!  Junior!

830
01:57:00,650 --> 01:57:02,850
Elsa...

831
01:57:03,750 --> 01:57:06,650
Don't, Elsa.

832
01:57:06,950 --> 01:57:09,050
Give me your other hand. I can't hold you.

833
01:57:09,350 --> 01:57:12,350
I can reach it. I can reach it.

834
01:57:12,550 --> 01:57:15,250
Give me your other hand!

835
01:57:16,950 --> 01:57:18,950
Elsa!

836
01:57:28,550 --> 01:57:32,050
Junior, give me your other hand. I can't hold on.

837
01:57:32,250 --> 01:57:35,650
I can get it. I can almost reach it, Dad.

838
01:57:36,550 --> 01:57:38,250
Indiana?

839
01:57:39,450 --> 01:57:41,650
Indiana...

840
01:57:43,950 --> 01:57:45,950
Let it go.

841
01:58:05,250 --> 01:58:06,550
Dad.

842
01:58:13,650 --> 01:58:15,150
Please, Dad.

843
01:58:46,750 --> 01:58:52,350
Elsa never really believed in the Grail. She thought she'd found a prize.

844
01:58:58,850 --> 01:59:00,850
What did you find, Dad?

845
01:59:01,050 --> 01:59:02,750
Me?

846
01:59:05,250 --> 01:59:07,550
Illumination.

847
01:59:16,750 --> 01:59:20,650
- And what did you find, Junior?  - "Junior"? Dad...

848
01:59:20,950 --> 01:59:22,850
What does it mean, this "Junior"?

849
01:59:23,150 --> 01:59:25,950
That's his name: Henry Jones Junior.

850
01:59:26,350 --> 01:59:29,850
- I like "Indiana". - We named the dog "Indiana"!

851
01:59:30,050 --> 01:59:32,150
May we go home now, please?

852
01:59:32,350 --> 01:59:36,850
The dog? You are named after the dog?

853
01:59:39,050 --> 01:59:42,650
I've got a lot of fond memories of that dog.

854
01:59:44,250 --> 01:59:46,450
- Ready?  - Ready.

855
01:59:46,650 --> 01:59:51,150
Indy, Henry, follow me!  I know the way!

856
01:59:55,750 --> 01:59:58,650
Got lost in his own museum, huh?

857
01:59:59,650 --> 02:00:01,850
After you, Junior.

858
02:00:02,150 --> 02:00:03,950
Yes, sir.

